ovascular accident"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bbd8e429202c","stem":"All but one of the followings are requirements to diagnose smouldering myeloma","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marrow plasmacytosis <30%"},{"label":"B","text":"IgG paraprotein of <70 gm/L"},{"label":"C","text":"IgA paraprotein of <50 gm/L"},{"label":"D","text":"No or < 3 lytic bone lesions E Urine light chain >4 gm/24 hours 135 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-782e1585ece2","stem":"All but one of the following criteria are required to make the diagnosis of M-GUS “Monoclonal Gammopathy of Undetermined Significance”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG of <35 gm/L"},{"label":"B","text":"IgA of <20 gm/L"},{"label":"C","text":"Urine light chain of < 1 gm/24 hours D Less than three lytic lesions"},{"label":"E","text":"Marrow plasmacytosis of <10%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9c9aea5ec3e6","stem":"The frequency of t(9;22) or what is known as Philadelphia chromosome in Adult Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"02-04%"},{"label":"B","text":"05-10%"},{"label":"C","text":"10-20% D 20-25%"},{"label":"E","text":"25-50%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f936530c10d7","stem":"Warfarin affects all but one of the clotting factors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factor II"},{"label":"B","text":"Factor VII"},{"label":"C","text":"Factor X"},{"label":"D","text":"Protein C E Factor V"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4b987bde375c","stem":"The frequency of t(9;22) or what is known as Philadelphia chromosome in Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in children is: A 02-04%","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"05-10%"},{"label":"C","text":"10-20%"},{"label":"D","text":"20-25%"},{"label":"E","text":"25-50%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f6f1ad110f8b","stem":"Washed cells are important in BT. Why? [Slide 5]","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a2b559a05106","stem":"When patient serum suspected to have immune antibodies in mixed with known O positive washed RBCs(antigen) and incubated at 37°C, washed then tested with Ant Human Globulin serum ( AHG) and the results shows agglutination. DAT Sensitized cells Indirect Coombs Test All of the above [Slide 20]","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-07bdf23c76ae","stem":"Mention methods of determination of ABO groups [Slide 23]","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0635fc17b568","stem":"Mention materials required to prepare sensitised cells [Slide 24]","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c4f06dd8e87a","stem":"Mention components of Crossmatch","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-54530c83abbd","stem":"Mention purpose of Normal saline in BT section","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5254f7e48343","stem":"Name the three genes responsible for the production of Rh antigens. RHAG, RH1, and RH2 RHAG, DCE, and dce RHAG, RHD, and RHCE RHD, RHCc, and RHEe RHD, RHCE, and RHce Sorry, that’s incorrect. In order to have normal Rh antigens on RBCs, all three genes must be present. RHD and RHCE are located on chromosome 1. RHD codes for the presence or absence of the D antigen. RHCE has 4 main alleles to cover the inheritance of C/c and E/e: RHCe, RHCE, RHce, and RHcE (Look closer! The difference is just the 4 combinations of C or c and E or e). RHAG (Rh associated glycoprotein) is located on chromosome 6. The presence of this gene allows the proteins resulting from RHD and RHCE to be incorporated into the RBC membrane. The absence of RHAG can result in a rare condition known as “Rhnull,” in which the patient has no Rh antigens of any type on their RBCs. Rhnull patients will typically have hemolytic anemia of varying severity, along with displaying unusual RBC shapes known as “stomatocytes” (“mouth cells”). This question was contributed by Bill Turcan and edited in 12/2017 by Joe Chaffin.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5a5b92b8b3f4","stem":"Which of the following is TRUE regarding the weak D phenotype?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1ccdd406fd87","stem":"Which of the following red blood cell abnormalities is associated with the Rhnull phenotype?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3c08f3182677","stem":"What percentage of blood specimens derived from those of European descent will have a positive agglutination result with the anti-c reagent? 15% 35% 50% 80% 100% CORRECT! This is a more obtuse way to ask the question, “What percentage of the Caucasian population is c positive?” 80% of Caucasians are positive for the c antigen. The c antigen is present in 96% of those of African descent, and 47% of those of Asian descent.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-33a9b945cb7f","stem":"Which alloantibody is most likely to be produced if a patient that has the Rh genotype of R1R1 is transfused with red blood cells that have an Rh genotype of R0R0?","type":"Essay / Structured Essay","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a2d7147b4ebc","stem":"Which of the following is TRUE about Anti-D?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-818aa0361cc0","stem":"Anti-G will react with red blood cells of each of the following phenotypes except: D+C- Sorry, that’s incorrect. Anti-G is an antibody that reacts, shockingly, with the G antigen (also known as “Rh12”). While that seems easy enough, what is not obvious is that the G antigen itself is actually the result of a common amino acid at position 103 of either the RHD or RHCE protein. Inheritance of the RHD allele at the RHD site, or inheritance of either or both of the RHCe and RHCE alleles at the RHCE site result in the presence of the serine at that position (for the RHD allele, on the RHD protein; for the RHCe and/or RHCE alleles, on the RHCE protein). As a result, G is best thought of as being present on an RBC that has the antigens D and/or C. Anti-G, then, will agglutinate cells that are positive for the D antigen only, the C antigen only, or both the D antigen and the C antigen. The only RBC phenotype that will show no agglutination is a cell that is both D negative and C negative (most D-negative cells are also G-negative due to the fact that most D-negative individuals have the genotype rr). (EXCEPTION: Rare cells have been described that are D-negative and C-negative but G-positive; i.e., they are D-C-G+. This scenario results from inheritance of a gene called rG [say it like this: “little r-G”]. Anti-G will show agglutination with these RBCs because the G antigen is present. This phenotype is not found on routine antibody panels). This question contributed by Bill Turcan, August 2013 and modified by Joe Chaffin, May 2016.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"D","text":"C+"},{"label":"D","text":"C- D+C+ rG"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","nonunique_option_labels","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cdbee328b4fa","stem":"The full blood count (FBC) and PBS tests can be used to investigate IDA. Which blood cells will be affected?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-506b7f781f10","stem":"What is the most significant feature of aplastic anemia?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d7f7f5bf68f6d9ceba2642070831a610bc78f661"},{"id":"q-6735183a999c","stem":"0g/dl What is the lowest acceptable hematocrit for female donors? 36% 38% 41% 43% Prospective donors who have malaria should be Permanently rejected Defferred for three years after cessation of treatment Defferred for two years after cessation of treatment Deferred for six months Symptom free donors who have been immunized with oral polio, measles or mumps vaccines are acceptable after a period of 24 hrs 24d days 2 weeks 4 weeks An autologous blood donor must Be over 21 years old Be treated for bacteremia Have at least an 11g/dl hemoglobin Be on oral iron therapy Which biochemical change does not occur in stored blood? Plasma pH decrease Plasma K decrease Plasma NH3 increase Inorganic phosphate increases What is the acceptable temperature range for blood bank refrigerator","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2147c88ee7cb","stem":"When the body is first exposed to a foreign antigen, it makes what is called a","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e59117cdabcd","stem":"Considering the three RBC antigens (A, B, and Rh), how many blood types are there?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-706c226aebfc","stem":"Which biochemical change does not occur in stored blood?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b90d0e36f4a5","stem":"What is the acceptable temperature range for blood bank refrigerator","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,